  "worked_solution": "First, let's find the differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202x})\\,\\text{d}x+(\\frac{\u2202f}{\u2202y})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\sqrt{x}+y)}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\sqrt{x}+y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point $({\\pi }^{2},0),$ the differential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{d}f(x,y) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi )}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\pi )\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x-\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+\u0394x,0+\u0394y),$ then we can approximate the change in $f$ as\n\n$$\n\u0394f\u2248-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\,\u0394x-\\,\u0394y\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2)=f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),\n$$\n\nso we want to move to the point $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2).$\n\nMoving from $({\\pi }^{2},0)$ to $({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394x & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)-{\\pi }^{2}=1, \\\\ \u0394y & =0.2-0=0.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u0394f & \u2248(-\\frac{1}{2\\pi })\\cdot (1)-1\\cdot (0.2) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\pi }-0.2 \\\\  & \u2248-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that the value of $f$ decreases by about $-0.359.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({\\pi }^{2}+1,0.2) & \u2248f({\\pi }^{2},0)-0.359 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}+1}+0.2) & \u2248\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\sqrt{{\\pi }^{2}}+0)-0.359 \\\\  & =0-0.359 \\\\  & =-0.359.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
